\paragraph{Where the advantage manifests.}
Adaptive reweighting does not accelerate training: the unweighted loss curves of adaptive and uniform runs overlap throughout (difference ${\sim}1.4\%$).
The advantage appears entirely at evaluation: adaptive achieves PPL 17.65 vs.\ uniform 17.69 on human-authored text.
This is consistent with the minimum intervention mechanism---the method reshapes the effective training distribution toward human-like text without materially changing the optimization trajectory.

\paragraph{$\tau$ as a practical dial.}

\paragraph{Why not a fixed $b$?}
The aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ might suggest that a fixed $b{=}1.4$ would suffice, but this average masks extreme per-batch heterogeneity (std $\approx$ 1.9, range $[0, 20]$).
A fixed $b{=}1.4$ would over-penalize the 55\% of batches needing only mild correction ($\bstar < 1$) while under-penalizing the 3.6\% requiring aggressive intervention ($\bstar > 5$).
The two binding regimes also differ: $\aeff$-binding batches have mean $\bstar{=}1.05$, while ESS-binding batches have mean $\bstar{=}1.64$---a fixed value is suboptimal for both.
The best fixed exponent from grid search ($b{=}0.5$ across seeds) validates this: it is far below the adaptive mean, and itself varies with contamination level (optimal $b{=}0.3$ at $\rho{=}0.4$ vs.\ $b{=}0.5$ at $\rho{=}0.6$), requiring per-setting retuning that the adaptive method eliminates.

